---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/content/FaqList.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CITATIONS } from '../data/citations';

const faqs = [
  {
    question:
      'Does choosing a low-impact or no-jumping workout make it medically safe for me?',
    answer:
      'No. Those labels describe exercise characteristics, not your individual readiness. Symptoms, health conditions, medications, pregnancy, recovery, and clinician instructions may change what is appropriate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should exercise feel uncomfortable?',
    answer:
      'Effort and temporary muscle fatigue can occur, but pain, dizziness, faintness, chest pressure, or unusual shortness of breath are not targets. Stop and seek appropriate help when symptoms concern you.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can WorkoutMatch filter around an injury?',
    answer:
      'It can omit broad movement characteristics you select, such as wrist loading or deep knee flexion, but that is only a preference filter. It cannot diagnose an injury or design rehabilitation.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Workout Safety and Readiness Guidance"
  description="Read practical pre-workout checks, stop signals, equipment guidance, and the limits of general WorkoutMatch exercise information."
  path="/safety/"
>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Safety' }]} />
  </div>

  <header class="narrow section-sm">
    <p class="eyebrow">Before you move</p>
    <h1>Workout safety</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      WorkoutMatch provides general exercise organization for adults. It cannot
      assess your health, observe your form, inspect your equipment, or
      determine whether a workout is appropriate for you.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="narrow safety-content">
    <section class="callout" aria-labelledby="urgent-heading">
      <h2 id="urgent-heading">Stop when something feels wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Stop exercising if you develop pain, dizziness, faintness, chest
        pressure, unusual shortness of breath, loss of control, or another
        concerning symptom. Seek urgent medical help for severe or emergency
        symptoms according to the services available where you are. This website
        cannot triage symptoms.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="before-heading">
      <h2 id="before-heading">Before a session</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Read the complete workout and each unfamiliar exercise before starting
          a timer.
        </li>
        <li>
          Clear trip hazards, allow enough overhead and side clearance, and
          choose a surface with reliable traction.
        </li>
        <li>
          Inspect bands for damage, verify pull-up bars and anchors according to
          their instructions, and keep chairs or benches from rolling or
          sliding.
        </li>
        <li>
          Choose a load you can lift into position and put down under control.
          Do not rely on dropping equipment.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep water and any personally needed supplies accessible without
          obstructing the movement area.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you have a health condition, are pregnant or postpartum, are
        returning after surgery or a long break, take medication that affects
        exercise response, or have symptoms you do not understand, consult an
        appropriately qualified clinician before relying on a general workout.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="during-heading">
      <h2 id="during-heading">During a session</h2>
      <div class="grid-cards">
        <article class="card card-pad">
          <h3>Control before quantity</h3>
          <p>
            Use a range and pace you can control. Fewer repetitions, a shorter
            interval, more rest, or an easier variation are all valid
            adjustments.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card-pad">
          <h3>Keep margin in the set</h3>
          <p>
            General sessions do not require training to failure. End a set
            before fatigue prevents a stable setup or deliberate repetition.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card card-pad">
          <h3>Respect the chosen constraint</h3>
          <p>
            If an exercise conflicts with available equipment, floor access,
            noise, impact, space, or a movement you selected to avoid, replace
            it or stop rather than ignoring the conflict.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="starting-heading">
      <h2 id="starting-heading">Start smaller when appropriate</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href={CITATIONS.whoActivity.url}>World Health Organization</a> says
        all physical activity counts and any amount is better than none. Its guidelines
        are broad public-health guidance, not a personal clearance or demand that
        a beginner immediately complete a large weekly target. A shorter workout,
        fewer rounds, lighter resistance, or an easy walk can be a reasonable starting
        amount when appropriate for the individual.
      </p>
      <p>
        Increase one variable at a time—such as repetitions, range, load, pace,
        or rounds—and leave enough recovery to repeat the movement with control.
        WorkoutMatch does not manage recovery, weekly volume, or long-term
        progression for you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="not-rehab-heading">
      <h2 id="not-rehab-heading">Preference filters are not rehabilitation</h2>
      <p>
        Generator options such as avoiding wrist loading, deep knee flexion,
        overhead work, higher lower-back loading, or frequent floor transitions
        conservatively remove exercise characteristics. They do not identify a
        cause, establish a contraindication, or prescribe treatment. Do not use
        the generator to test an injury or work around professional
        restrictions.
      </p>
      <p>
        For source context and generator rules, read the <a
          href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}methodology/`}>methodology</a
        >. When a general session is suitable, you can <a
          href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}generate/`}>build a workout</a
        > or choose a
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}workouts/`}>curated example</a> after
        reading its limitations.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <div class="narrow section-sm">
    <FaqList items={faqs} heading="Safety questions" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .safety-content > section + section {
    margin-top: clamp(2.75rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .safety-content p,
  .safety-content li,
  .safety-content article p {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .safety-content li + li {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
  }

  .safety-content .callout h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  }

  .safety-content .callout p:last-child,
  .safety-content article p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
